now  okay so we de-energized uh the mount  charleston area at approximately 6 45  this morning that was based off of a  concern that the storm system that's  moving through the spring mountains  is bringing with it elevated winds we  were seeing winds in the kyle canyon  area  of about 65 miles an hour and up on the  angel peak area where a lot of the lines  that feed into mount charleston are we  were seeing  wind gusts in excess of 75 miles an hour  so at those elevated wind speeds  we definitely meet our psom criteria for  proactive de-energization and so that's  why we de-energized the mountain this  morning we've been tracking this weather  system for the past several days  uh in the past 36 hours we really saw a  change in the weather forecast an  increase in the forecasted wind gusts  specifically  and so that's why yesterday we declared  a psalm warning  once we realized that that forecast was  starting to change for the worse  specifically with the wind gusts  so right now we are de-energized  we expect wind gusts to peak somewhere  between 1 pm and 5 p.m today  and then depending on when those wind  gusts start to subside we will start the  re-energization of the mountain  prior to re-energization we have to  conduct a patrol  on  all of our facilities up in the mount  charleston area so if the winds do not  subside by say five or six tonight we  will have to keep the power off  overnight at mount charleston because we  have to conduct those inspections during  daylight hours one so that we can  actually see the facilities but two  to ensure the safety of our crews so we  have a full team up there right now that  continues to monitor the weather real  time and as soon as this weather event  subsides we will begin that proactive  re-energization process either tonight  if  if  the weather allows or tomorrow morning

